DSA -- Sorting and Searching
Introduction
Sorting and searching are important operations in Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA).
Sorting: Arranging data in a particular order, usually ascending or descending.
Searching: Finding a required element from a collection of data.
1. Sorting
Sorting is the process of arranging elements in a specific order.
Types of Sorting Algorithms
1. Bubble Sort
Bubble Sort compares two adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. This process continues until the array is sorted.
Main operation: Compare and Swap
2. Selection Sort
Selection Sort finds the smallest element from the unsorted part and places it at the correct position.
Main operation: Select Minimum and Swap
3. Insertion Sort
Insertion Sort takes one element at a time and inserts it into its correct position in the sorted part.
Main operation: Select and Insert
4. Merge Sort
Merge Sort divides the array into smaller parts, sorts those parts, and then merges them into one sorted array.
Main operation: Divide and Merge
5. Quick Sort
Quick Sort selects a pivot element and divides the array into two parts. Elements smaller than the pivot are placed on one side and larger elements on the other side.
Main operation: Pivot and Partition
6. Heap Sort
Heap Sort uses a heap data structure to arrange elements. It repeatedly takes the largest element and places it at the end.
Main operation: Build Heap and Delete Root
7. Radix Sort
Radix Sort sorts numbers digit by digit, starting from the least significant digit or the most significant digit.
Main operation: Sort by Digits
Sorting Complexity
Algorithm            Best Case   Average Case    Worst Case
Bubble Sort*             O(n)          O(n²)         O(n²) Selection Sort           O(n²)          O(n²)         O(n²) Insertion Sort            O(n)          O(n²)         O(n²) Merge Sort          O(n log n)     O(n log n)    O(n log n) Quick Sort          O(n log n)     O(n log n)         O(n²) Heap Sort           O(n log n)     O(n log n)    O(n log n) Radix Sort         O(d(n + b))    O(d(n + b))   O(d(n + b))
n = number of elements, d = number of digits, b = digit base.
Bubble Sort best case O(n) when an optimized version checks whether any swap occurred.
2. Searching
Searching is the process of finding the location of a particular element in a data collection.
Binary Search
Binary Search is a searching technique that repeatedly divides a sorted array into two halves to find the required element.
Main operations: 1. Find the middle element. 2. Compare the middle element with the search value. 3. If both are equal, the element is found. 4. If the search value is smaller, search the left half. 5. If the search value is greater, search the right half. 6. Repeat until the element is found or the search range becomes empty.
Time Complexity: - Best Case: O(1) - Average Case: O(log n) - Worst Case: O(log n)
Important: Binary Search works only on sorted data.
3. Basic Terms
Array: A collection of elements stored in continuous memory locations.
Element: A single data item in a collection.
Index: The position of an element in an array. In C++, array indexing starts from 0.
Ascending Order: Arranging elements from smallest to largest.
Descending Order: Arranging elements from largest to smallest.
Time Complexity: The amount of time an algorithm takes as the input size increases.
Space Complexity: The amount of extra memory an algorithm requires.
Stable Sorting: A sorting method that keeps equal elements in their original relative order.
In-place Sorting: A sorting method that uses very little extra memory.
4. Common Operations
Sorting Operations
Compare elements.
Swap elements.
Insert an element.
Select an element.
Divide data into smaller parts.
Merge sorted parts.
Partition data around a pivot.
Build and adjust a heap.
Searching Operations
Read the search element.
Compare elements.
Check the middle position.
Move to the left or right part.
Return the position if found.
Report when the element is not found.
5. Quick Revision
Topic            Main Point
Bubble Sort      Adjacent comparison and swapping Selection Sort   Select the minimum element Insertion Sort   Insert at the correct position Merge Sort       Divide and merge Quick Sort       Pivot and partition Heap Sort        Uses a heap Radix Sort       Sorts digit by digit Binary Search    Searches by dividing a sorted array into halves
